  "response": "I've created `/app/filter.py`. Running `python3 /app/filter.py file.html` strips the JavaScript and rewrites the file in place; everything else stays byte-for-byte the same.\n\n**How it works:** it reads the HTML the same way a browser does, using its own parser rather than BeautifulSoup, because BeautifulSoup would reformat the file. It only cuts out the dangerous parts and copies everything else exactly, including whitespace, line endings, quoting, case and comments. If nothing needed removing, the file isn't rewritten.\n\n**What gets removed:**\n- `<script>` elements and their contents, plus any leftover `<script` text, such as what remains after `<scr<script>ipt>` tricks.\n- Event-handler attributes (`onload`, `onerror`, `ontoggle`, …) on any tag. This includes SVG `attributeName=\"on…\"`.\n- `srcdoc` attributes.\n- Attributes containing `javascript:`, `vbscript:` or similar script links. This still works when the text is disguised with HTML character codes, tabs or control characters.\n- `data:` links in link and source attributes, except images, audio, video and fonts (SVG images only on `<img>`).\n- `style` attributes and `<style>` blocks that use `expression(`, `behavior:`, `-moz-binding` or `javascript:`, even when disguised with CSS escapes or comments.\n\n**Tricky cases also handled:**\n- Text inside `<title>`, `<textarea>`, `<noscript>` and `<style>` is also checked as if it were HTML. This catches attacks that rely on the filter and the browser reading the same text differently.\n- Comment contents are cleaned too, e.g. old IE conditional comments.\n\n**Testing (inside the container):**\n- **Normal HTML:** a sample page with tables, headers, forms, `<style>`, comments, character codes and a `data:image` came back byte-identical.\n- **Attacks:** I ran 42 common XSS attacks in headless Chromium through Selenium. Before filtering, 29 of them raised an alert; after filtering, none did.\n\n**Trade-offs:**\n- Any attribute containing the text `javascript:` is removed, even a harmless one like a `title`.\n- Script-like text inside `<textarea>` or comments is also removed, though browsers wouldn't run it there.\n\nI chose to over-remove in these rare cases rather than risk missing an attack.",
